{
  "schemaVersion": 3,
  "id": "still",
  "name": "Still",
  "tagline": "Drift · no beat: one open chord, held, a low drone, a dark wide room.",
  "description": "No pulse at all. An open minor chord held by four voices, a low drone on the root, the root's harmonics swelling far above, a little pitched air, all in a large dark room. Every few minutes one voice slowly crossfades to a neighbouring note; nothing starts, nothing stops, nothing is melodic.",
  "use": {
    "name": "Read",
    "for": "Reading and close study: following an argument on the page.",
    "why": "The main cost of background sound to verbal work is spectral change between successive pieces (speech, melody, separate hits); a stationary bed and a repeated identical token cost nothing measurable, and playback level does not matter (research rules 2 and 3). For reading comprehension the realistic best is no harm, not help (rule 4). So Still has no pulse and no notes: one chord whose voices change rarely and only by slow crossfade, below the rate at which anything reads as an event. Evidence: strong for the mechanism; nobody has tested a beatless bed against Pulse-style beats on reading."
  },
